Task 1: Install and Configure HashiCorp Vault
Subtask 1.1: Install Vault
First, we need to install HashiCorp Vault on our Linux machine.

Update the system package manager:
sudo apt update
Install required dependencies:
sudo apt install -y wget unzip curl
Download the latest Vault binary:
wget https://releases.hashicorp.com/vault/1.15.2/vault_1.15.2_linux_amd64.zip
Extract the Vault binary:
unzip vault_1.15.2_linux_amd64.zip
Move Vault to a directory in your PATH:
sudo mv vault /usr/local/bin/
Verify the installation:
vault version
You should see output similar to:

Vault v1.15.2 (build date)
Subtask 1.2: Create Vault Configuration
Create a directory for Vault configuration and data:
sudo mkdir -p /opt/vault/data
sudo mkdir -p /opt/vault/config
Create a Vault configuration file:
sudo tee /opt/vault/config/vault.hcl > /dev/null <<EOF
storage "file" {
  path = "/opt/vault/data"
}

listener "tcp" {
  address = "127.0.0.1:8200"
  tls_disable = true
}

ui = true
disable_mlock = true
EOF
Set appropriate permissions:
sudo chown -R $USER:$USER /opt/vault
Subtask 1.3: Start Vault Server
Start the Vault server in development mode for this lab:
vault server -config=/opt/vault/config/vault.hcl &
Set the Vault address environment variable:
export VAULT_ADDR='http://127.0.0.1:8200'
Verify the server is running:
vault status
You should see that the server is running but sealed.

Task 2: Initialize and Unseal Vault
Subtask 2.1: Initialize Vault
Initialize Vault with 5 key shares and a threshold of 3:
vault operator init -key-shares=5 -key-threshold=3
Important: Save the unseal keys and root token that are displayed. You will need them for the next steps.

Example output:

Unseal Key 1: [key1]
Unseal Key 2: [key2]
Unseal Key 3: [key3]
Unseal Key 4: [key4]
Unseal Key 5: [key5]

Initial Root Token: [root_token]
Store the root token in an environment variable (replace with your actual token):
export VAULT_TOKEN="your_root_token_here"
Subtask 2.2: Unseal Vault
Unseal Vault using three of the five unseal keys:
vault operator unseal [unseal_key_1]
vault operator unseal [unseal_key_2]
vault operator unseal [unseal_key_3]
Verify Vault is unsealed:
vault status
You should see Sealed: false in the output.

Task 3: Store and Retrieve Secrets Using KV Secrets Engine
Subtask 3.1: Enable KV Version 1 Secrets Engine
Enable the KV version 1 secrets engine at the path secret-v1:
vault secrets enable -path=secret-v1 kv
List enabled secrets engines to verify:
vault secrets list
Subtask 3.2: Store Secrets in KV Version 1
Store a simple database credential:
vault kv put secret-v1/database username=admin password=secretpass123
Store application configuration secrets:
vault kv put secret-v1/app/config \
  api_key=abc123def456 \
  database_url=postgresql://localhost:5432/myapp \
  debug_mode=false
Store multiple environment secrets:
vault kv put secret-v1/environments/production \
  db_host=prod-db.company.com \
  db_user=prod_user \
  db_password=prod_secure_pass \
  redis_url=redis://prod-redis:6379
Subtask 3.3: Retrieve Secrets from KV Version 1
Retrieve the database credentials:
vault kv get secret-v1/database
Retrieve specific fields from the database secret:
vault kv get -field=username secret-v1/database
vault kv get -field=password secret-v1/database
Retrieve secrets in JSON format:
vault kv get -format=json secret-v1/app/config
List all secrets under a path:
vault kv list secret-v1/
vault kv list secret-v1/environments/
Subtask 3.4: Update and Delete Secrets in KV Version 1
Update the database password:
vault kv put secret-v1/database username=admin password=newpassword456
Verify the update:
vault kv get secret-v1/database
Delete a specific secret:
vault kv delete secret-v1/app/config
Verify the deletion:
vault kv get secret-v1/app/config
You should receive an error indicating the secret doesn't exist.

Task 4: Implement Versioned Secrets with KV Version 2
Subtask 4.1: Enable KV Version 2 Secrets Engine
Enable the KV version 2 secrets engine:
vault secrets enable -path=secret-v2 kv-v2
Configure the secrets engine with version limits:
vault write secret-v2/config max_versions=10 delete_version_after="72h"
Verify the configuration:
vault read secret-v2/config
Subtask 4.2: Store Versioned Secrets
Create the first version of an application secret:
vault kv put secret-v2/myapp/database \
  host=localhost \
  port=5432 \
  username=appuser \
  password=version1pass
Create a second version with updated credentials:
vault kv put secret-v2/myapp/database \
  host=db.example.com \
  port=5432 \
  username=appuser \
  password=version2pass \
  ssl_mode=require
Create a third version with additional configuration:
vault kv put secret-v2/myapp/database \
  host=db.example.com \
  port=5432 \
  username=newuser \
  password=version3pass \
  ssl_mode=require \
  connection_pool=20
Subtask 4.3: Retrieve Versioned Secrets
Get the latest version (version 3):
vault kv get secret-v2/myapp/database
Get a specific version (version 1):
vault kv get -version=1 secret-v2/myapp/database
Get version 2:
vault kv get -version=2 secret-v2/myapp/database
View metadata for all versions:
vault kv metadata get secret-v2/myapp/database
Subtask 4.4: Manage Secret Versions
Create additional secrets for version management demonstration:
vault kv put secret-v2/api/keys \
  primary_key=key_v1 \
  secondary_key=backup_v1

vault kv put secret-v2/api/keys \
  primary_key=key_v2 \
  secondary_key=backup_v2 \
  tertiary_key=extra_v2

vault kv put secret-v2/api/keys \
  primary_key=key_v3 \
  secondary_key=backup_v3 \
  tertiary_key=extra_v3
List all versions:
vault kv metadata get secret-v2/api/keys
Delete a specific version (version 2):
vault kv delete -versions=2 secret-v2/api/keys
Verify the deletion:
vault kv get -version=2 secret-v2/api/keys
Undelete the version:
vault kv undelete -versions=2 secret-v2/api/keys
Verify the restoration:
vault kv get -version=2 secret-v2/api/keys
Subtask 4.5: Advanced Version Management
Destroy a version permanently (cannot be recovered):
vault kv destroy -versions=1 secret-v2/api/keys
Try to retrieve the destroyed version:
vault kv get -version=1 secret-v2/api/keys
Rollback to a previous version by creating a new version with old data:
# First, get the data from version 2
vault kv get -version=2 -format=json secret-v2/api/keys

# Then create a new version with that data
vault kv put secret-v2/api/keys \
  primary_key=key_v2 \
  secondary_key=backup_v2 \
  tertiary_key=extra_v2
Set metadata for better secret management:
vault kv metadata put -max-versions=5 secret-v2/api/keys
vault kv metadata put -delete-version-after=24h secret-v2/myapp/database
Task 5: Practical Secret Management Scenarios
Subtask 5.1: Database Connection Management
Create a comprehensive database configuration:
vault kv put secret-v2/databases/production \
  host=prod-db-cluster.company.com \
  port=5432 \
  database=production_app \
  username=app_prod_user \
  password=super_secure_prod_password \
  ssl_mode=require \
  connection_timeout=30 \
  max_connections=100
Create a staging environment configuration:
vault kv put secret-v2/databases/staging \
  host=staging-db.company.com \
  port=5432 \
  database=staging_app \
  username=app_staging_user \
  password=staging_password_123 \
  ssl_mode=prefer \
  connection_timeout=15 \
  max_connections=50
Retrieve database configurations for different environments:
vault kv get -field=host secret-v2/databases/production
vault kv get -field=username secret-v2/databases/staging
Subtask 5.2: API Key Management
Store API keys for external services:
vault kv put secret-v2/external-apis/payment-gateway \
  api_key=pk_live_abc123def456ghi789 \
  secret_key=sk_live_xyz987uvw654rst321 \
  webhook_secret=whsec_webhook123secret456 \
  environment=production

vault kv put secret-v2/external-apis/email-service \
  api_key=SG.email_api_key_here.more_key_data \
  sender_email=noreply@company.com \
  sender_name="Company Name" \
  rate_limit=1000
Update API keys (simulating key rotation):
vault kv put secret-v2/external-apis/payment-gateway \
  api_key=pk_live_new123key456here789 \
  secret_key=sk_live_new987key654here321 \
  webhook_secret=whsec_newwebhook123secret456 \
  environment=production \
  rotated_date="2024-01-15"
Retrieve the current and previous API keys:
vault kv get secret-v2/external-apis/payment-gateway
vault kv get -version=1 secret-v2/external-apis/payment-gateway
Subtask 5.3: Certificate and SSL Management
Store SSL certificate information:
vault kv put secret-v2/certificates/web-server \
  certificate="-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----
MIIDXTCCAkWgAwIBAgIJAKoK/heBjcOuMA0GCSqGSIb3DQEBBQUAMEUxCzAJBgNV
[certificate content would go here]
-----END CERTIFICATE-----" \
  private_key="-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----
MIIEvQIBADANBgkqhkiG9w0BAQEFAASCBKcwggSjAgEAAoIBAQC7VJTUt9Us8cKB
[private key content would go here]
-----END PRIVATE KEY-----" \
  expiry_date="2025-12-31" \
  issuer="Let's Encrypt"
Store certificate metadata separately:
vault kv put secret-v2/certificates/metadata/web-server \
  domain=www.company.com \
  san_domains="company.com,api.company.com" \
  issued_date="2024-01-01" \
  expiry_date="2025-12-31" \
  auto_renew=true
Task 6: Secret Engine Comparison and Best Practices
Subtask 6.1: Compare KV Version 1 and Version 2
Create the same secret in both engines:
# KV v1
vault kv put secret-v1/comparison \
  service=web-app \
  environment=production \
  version=1.0

# KV v2
vault kv put secret-v2/comparison \
  service=web-app \
  environment=production \
  version=1.0
Update both secrets:
# KV v1 - overwrites completely
vault kv put secret-v1/comparison \
  service=web-app \
  environment=production \
  version=2.0

# KV v2 - creates new version
vault kv put secret-v2/comparison \
  service=web-app \
  environment=production \
  version=2.0
Try to retrieve old versions:
# KV v1 - old version is gone
vault kv get secret-v1/comparison

# KV v2 - can access both versions
vault kv get -version=1 secret-v2/comparison
vault kv get -version=2 secret-v2/comparison
Subtask 6.2: Implement Secret Policies
Create a policy file for read-only access:
tee readonly-policy.hcl > /dev/null <<EOF
path "secret-v2/data/myapp/*" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}

path "secret-v2/metadata/myapp/*" {
  capabilities = ["read", "list"]
}
EOF
Create the policy in Vault:
vault policy write readonly-policy readonly-policy.hcl
Create a policy for full access to specific paths:
tee app-admin-policy.hcl > /dev/null <<EOF
path "secret-v2/data/myapp/*" {
  capabilities = ["create", "read", "update", "delete", "list"]
}

path "secret-v2/metadata/myapp/*" {
  capabilities = ["create", "read", "update", "delete", "list"]
}

path "secret-v2/delete/myapp/*" {
  capabilities = ["update"]
}

path "secret-v2/undelete/myapp/*" {
  capabilities = ["update"]
}

path "secret-v2/destroy/myapp/*" {
  capabilities = ["update"]
}
EOF
Create the admin policy:
vault policy write app-admin-policy app-admin-policy.hcl
List all policies:
vault policy list
